 About: Ports                                                                                       
   PORT            The number other programs connect to. A port can only have one listener per      
                   address, so "address already in use" means something here already has it: search 
                   for the port (/) to see what.                                                    
   PROTO           TCP for connections (web servers, databases, SSH). UDP for messages without a    
                   connection (DNS, discovery, games, video calls).                                 
   ADDRESS         Where it listens. 0.0.0.0 or :: means every network interface, so other machines 
                   can reach it unless a firewall blocks it. 127.0.0.1 or ::1 means this machine    
                   only.                                                                            
   PID / PROCESS   The process that owns the socket. Enter goes to it on the Processes tab; d/D     
                   ends it. (unknown) means the OS didn't say: on Linux, other users' sockets need  
                   root (sudo) to see their owner.                                                  
   Listening       Waiting for others to connect: TCP sockets in LISTEN, and UDP sockets bound to a 
                   port. Press . to show established connections too.                               
                                                                                                    
 Keys: Ports                                                                                        
   /        search by port, name, PID or address                                                    
   d/D      end / force kill the owner (t: also processes it started)                               
   s/S      next sort column / reverse                                                              
   .        show connections too, not just listening                                                
   enter    go to the owning process                                                                
                                                                                                    
 Keys: Everywhere                                                                                   
   j/k      move down / up                                                                          
   g/G      top / bottom                                                                            
   ^d/^u    half page down / up                                                                     
   1-3      jump to tab                                                                             
 esc close j/k scroll  lines 1–27 of 34